WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.443
Two hinges per residue sounds like a lot of freedom.

2
00:00:07.403 --> 00:00:09.183
How many of those settings actually work?

3
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:11.513
Take two linked cards.

4
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:14.273
Hold one hinge still, and turn the other.

5
00:00:15.283 --> 00:00:19.403
At many settings, an atom on one card runs into an atom on the next.

6
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.293
A C=O group comes too close to an N–H group.

7
00:00:23.813 --> 00:00:26.093
Or a side chain runs into the backbone.

8
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.453
The atoms would have to sit in the same space.

9
00:00:30.293 --> 00:00:32.053
Those settings are strongly disfavored.

10
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:34.943
Now try every pair of angles.

11
00:00:35.823 --> 00:00:38.043
Put phi along the bottom and psi up the side.

12
00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:43.277
Each runs from minus −180° degrees to plus +180°.

13
00:00:43.277 --> 00:00:44.277
degrees.

14
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.870
Shade every pair that does not clash.

15
00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.850
G. N. Ramachandran did this calculation.

16
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:55.873
The survivors cluster into a few compact islands.

17
00:00:56.753 --> 00:00:58.073
The rest of the map is empty.

18
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:02.353
Only a small fraction of the map is open to the backbone.

19
00:01:03.293 --> 00:01:04.813
This is the Ramachandran plot.

20
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:10.177
Here is the test. Put both angles at zero, right in the center.

21
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:12.117
Will that pair survive?

22
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:23.280
No. The atoms of neighbouring peptide planes run into each other.

23
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.460
The center of the map is empty.

24
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:27.753
Now read the islands.

25
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:32.773
The broad one at the upper left is for extended strands, like the beta sheet.

26
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.867
The compact one at the lower left is the right-handed alpha helix.

27
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:41.557
A small island at the upper right is left-handed.

28
00:01:42.457 --> 00:01:44.117
It is used mostly by glycine.

29
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:48.110
Glycine roams more widely than any other residue.

30
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.290
Its side chain is a single hydrogen, so it bumps into less.

31
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:55.273
Each island matches a real shape.

32
00:01:56.153 --> 00:01:57.513
The helix island builds a coil.

33
00:01:58.433 --> 00:02:00.233
The strand island builds a stretched chain.

34
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:03.497
The map is also a check on new structures.

35
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.137
Plot every residue's two angles.

36
00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.490
If the points land on the islands, the structure is plausible.

37
00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.530
If many stray, something is probably wrong.

38
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.710
Forbidden means strongly disfavored, not impossible.

39
00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.970
A few residues sit in strained spots, held by their surroundings.

40
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.480
Those outliers are often the ones that matter for catalysis.

41
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.060
Most hinge settings crash.

42
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.440
The backbone is left with only a few local shapes.
